1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:04,937
The brush clears a crown, the type worn by Greek-style kings.

2
00:00:04,937 --> 00:00:12,043
It belongs to rulers who came from Bactria, across the Hindu Kush, and made their home in north-western India.

3
00:00:12,043 --> 00:00:19,280
Their coins show Greek faces and Indian words. Why does a Greek king appear on an Indian coin?

4
00:00:34,317 --> 00:00:41,090
How do we know? Mostly from coins. Their coins carry names in Greek and in Kharosthi.

5
00:00:41,090 --> 00:00:50,665
The Junagadh inscription records Rudradaman, a Shaka ruler. The Milindapanha, a Buddhist text, records a talk with King Menander.

6
00:01:04,183 --> 00:01:12,365
After the Mauryas, the north-west was open to foreign rulers. The first were the Indo-Greeks, also called the Bactrian Greeks.

7
00:01:12,365 --> 00:01:22,180
Demetrius was among the earliest, about 200 BCE. The most famous Indo-Greek king was Menander, called Milinda in Indian texts.

8
00:01:22,180 --> 00:01:30,396
The Milindapanha, a Buddhist text, records his questions to the monk Nagasena. Indo-Greek coins were fine and clear.

9
00:01:30,396 --> 00:01:35,833
They carry the ruler's portrait and a legend in Greek on one side, and in Kharosthi on the other.

10
00:01:35,833 --> 00:01:44,035
They were the first coins that can be firmly credited to named kings. Next came the Shakas, who came from Central Asia.

11
00:01:44,035 --> 00:01:53,239
Their best-known ruler was Rudradaman, about 150 CE. His Junagadh inscription is the first long inscription in classical Sanskrit.

12
00:01:53,239 --> 00:02:02,392
It records the repair of the Sudarshana lake. The Parthians, or Pahlavas, came after them. Gondophernes is their most famous king.

13
00:02:02,392 --> 00:02:09,202
The Shaka era, which starts in 78 CE, is still used in the Indian national calendar.

14
00:02:22,717 --> 00:02:31,779
Old name, new name. Indian texts called the Greeks Yavanas. The word is thought to come from Ionians, a Greek people.

15
00:02:31,779 --> 00:02:39,360
Today, the word Greek is used. Yavana was used in India for foreigners from the west, for a long time.

16
00:02:52,883 --> 00:03:03,963
Carve this tip into memory. Greeks: coins, Shakas: Sanskrit. Parthians: Gondophernes. One mark for each foreign people.

17
00:03:03,963 --> 00:03:07,474
Coins, Sanskrit and a king's name.

18
00:03:21,017 --> 00:03:36,823
Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. Number one. Which Indo-Greek king is called Milinda in Indian texts? The answer: Menander.

19
00:03:36,823 --> 00:03:49,439
Number two. Which Shaka ruler issued the Junagadh inscription? The answer: Rudradaman. Number three.

20
00:03:49,439 --> 00:04:01,786
Which two scripts appear on Indo-Greek coins? The answer: Greek and Kharosthi. Now one line for Mains.

21
00:04:01,786 --> 00:04:07,258
Evaluate the contribution of foreign rulers to Indian culture after the Mauryas.

22
00:04:24,783 --> 00:04:32,515
The crown goes into its case. It stands for the rulers who came from across the mountains. Three facts to take away.

23
00:04:32,515 --> 00:04:44,323
A Greek-style crown of Bactria. Indo-Greeks, Shakas, Parthians. Greek and Kharosthi on their coins. Next gallery: The Kushanas.

